{
 "name": "tideWater",
 "description": "Water read from underneath. A wave photographs well because its lip is translucent: light behind it comes through where the water is thin, and the thin part is exactly where the surface turns away from the eye -- so fresnel is that measurement, and it is the whole reason this material exists. A facing gradient separates water lit from the surface above from water in its own shadow, so the form is described rather than flat, and two decorrelated world-space noise scales drifting at different rates put caustics on it that crawl instead of sitting still.",
